WEBVTT

00:00.610 --> 00:02.390
In the last video, we
took our first look

00:02.390 --> 00:04.889
at the epsilon-delta definition
of limits, which essentially

00:04.889 --> 00:08.800
says if you claim that the limit
of f of x as x approaches C

00:08.800 --> 00:13.020
is equal to L, then that
must mean by the definition

00:13.020 --> 00:16.250
that if you were given any
positive epsilon that it

00:16.250 --> 00:20.010
essentially tells us how close
we want f of x to be to L.

00:20.010 --> 00:23.152
We can always find
a delta that's

00:23.152 --> 00:25.110
greater than 0, which is
essentially telling us

00:25.110 --> 00:30.050
our distance from C such that
if x is within delta of C,

00:30.050 --> 00:34.285
then f of x is going to
be within epsilon of L.

00:34.285 --> 00:37.530
If we can find a
delta for any epsilon,

00:37.530 --> 00:41.310
then we can say
that this is indeed

00:41.310 --> 00:43.715
the limit of f of x
as x approaches C.

00:43.715 --> 00:45.090
Now, I know what
you're thinking.

00:45.090 --> 00:47.250
This seems all very abstract.

00:47.250 --> 00:49.530
I want to somehow
use this thing.

00:49.530 --> 00:51.660
And what we will do in
this video is use it

00:51.660 --> 00:56.040
and to rigorously prove that
a limit actually exists.

00:56.040 --> 00:59.400
So, right over here, I've
defined a function, f of x.

00:59.400 --> 01:03.850
It's equal to 2x everywhere
except for x equals 5.

01:03.850 --> 01:06.960
So it's 2x everywhere for
all the other values of x,

01:06.960 --> 01:09.340
but when x is equal to
5, it's just equal to x.

01:09.340 --> 01:12.750
So I could have
really just written 5.

01:12.750 --> 01:15.430
It's equal to 5 when
x is equal to 5.

01:15.430 --> 01:16.370
It's equal to itself.

01:16.370 --> 01:18.420
And so we've drawn
the graph here.

01:18.420 --> 01:20.760
Everywhere else, it
looks just like 2x.

01:20.760 --> 01:24.360
At x is equal to 5, it's
not along the line 2x.

01:24.360 --> 01:26.580
Instead, the function
is defined to be

01:26.580 --> 01:28.610
that point right over there.

01:28.610 --> 01:33.660
And if I were to ask you
what is the limit of f

01:33.660 --> 01:35.799
of x as x approaches
5, you might

01:35.799 --> 01:37.090
think of it pretty intuitively.

01:37.090 --> 01:37.756
Well, let's see.

01:37.756 --> 01:41.930
The closer I get
to 5, the closer

01:41.930 --> 01:44.740
f of x seems to
be getting to 10.

01:50.090 --> 01:53.380
And so, you might fairly
intuitively make the claim

01:53.380 --> 01:58.550
that the limit of f of x
as x approaches 5 really

01:58.550 --> 01:59.890
is equal to 10.

01:59.890 --> 02:01.140
It looks that way.

02:01.140 --> 02:02.980
But what we're
going to do is use

02:02.980 --> 02:08.080
the epsilon-delta definition
to actually prove it.

02:08.080 --> 02:11.030
And the way that most of
these proofs typically go

02:11.030 --> 02:13.330
is we define delta
in the abstract.

02:13.330 --> 02:15.500
And then we essentially
try to come up with a way

02:15.500 --> 02:20.590
that given any epsilon, we can
always come up with a delta.

02:20.590 --> 02:22.110
Or another way is
we're going to try

02:22.110 --> 02:26.510
to describe our delta as
a function of epsilon,

02:26.510 --> 02:28.230
not to confuse you too much.

02:28.230 --> 02:29.810
But maybe I shouldn't
use f again.

02:29.810 --> 02:35.520
But delta equals
function of epsilon

02:35.520 --> 02:37.770
that is defined for
any positive epsilon.

02:37.770 --> 02:39.960
So you give me an
epsilon, I just

02:39.960 --> 02:42.400
put into our little formula
or little function box.

02:42.400 --> 02:44.070
And I will always
get you a delta.

02:44.070 --> 02:46.790
And if I can do that for any
epsilon that'll always give you

02:46.790 --> 02:52.600
a delta, where this is true,
that if x is within delta of C,

02:52.600 --> 02:55.180
then the corresponding f of x
is going to be within epsilon

02:55.180 --> 02:57.870
of L. Then the limit
definitely exists.

02:57.870 --> 03:00.090
So let's try to do that.

03:00.090 --> 03:05.490
So let's think about being
within delta of our C.

03:05.490 --> 03:09.070
So, let's think about this
right over here is 5 plus delta,

03:09.070 --> 03:11.312
this is 5 minus delta.

03:11.312 --> 03:13.270
So that's our range we're
going to think about.

03:13.270 --> 03:15.170
We're going to think about
it in the abstract at first.

03:15.170 --> 03:16.753
And then we're going
to try to come up

03:16.753 --> 03:19.600
with a formula for delta
in terms of epsilon.

03:19.600 --> 03:23.750
So how could we describe all of
the x's that are in this range

03:23.750 --> 03:25.216
but not equal to 5 itself?

03:25.216 --> 03:26.840
Because we really
care about the things

03:26.840 --> 03:30.310
that are within delta of 5,
but not necessarily equal to 5.

03:30.310 --> 03:32.400
This is just a
strictly less than.

03:32.400 --> 03:35.940
They're within a range
of C, but not equal to C.

03:35.940 --> 03:40.950
Well, that's going to be all of
the x's that satisfy x minus 5

03:40.950 --> 03:44.460
is less than delta.

03:44.460 --> 03:48.000
That describes all of
these x's right over here.

03:48.000 --> 03:51.250
And now, what we're going to
do, and the way these proofs

03:51.250 --> 03:53.030
typically go, is
we're going to try

03:53.030 --> 03:57.100
to manipulate the left-hand
side of the inequality,

03:57.100 --> 03:59.180
so it starts to look
something like this,

03:59.180 --> 04:01.350
or it starts to look
exactly like that.

04:01.350 --> 04:03.570
And as we do that,
the right-hand side

04:03.570 --> 04:06.560
of the inequality is going to
be expressed in terms of delta.

04:06.560 --> 04:08.730
And then we can
essentially say well, look.

04:08.730 --> 04:11.771
If the right-hand
side is in terms

04:11.771 --> 04:13.520
of delta and the
left-hand side looks just

04:13.520 --> 04:16.880
like that, that really defines
how we can express delta

04:16.880 --> 04:18.560
in terms of epsilon.

04:18.560 --> 04:20.360
If that doesn't make
sense, bear with me.

04:20.360 --> 04:21.690
I'm about to do it.

04:21.690 --> 04:26.930
So, if we want x minus 5 to
look a lot more like this, when

04:26.930 --> 04:30.780
x is not equal to 5-- in all
of this, this whole interval,

04:30.780 --> 04:35.520
x is not equal to 5--
f of x is equal to 2x,

04:35.520 --> 04:37.280
our proposed limit
is equal to 10.

04:37.280 --> 04:40.250
So if we could somehow get
this to be 2x minus 10,

04:40.250 --> 04:41.515
then we're in good shape.

04:41.515 --> 04:42.890
And the easiest
way to do that is

04:42.890 --> 04:45.730
to multiply both sides
of this inequality by 2.

04:50.750 --> 04:52.960
And 2 times the absolute
value of something,

04:52.960 --> 04:55.760
that's the same thing as the
absolute value of 2 times

04:55.760 --> 04:56.300
that thing.

04:56.300 --> 04:59.300
If I were to say 2 times
the absolute value of a,

04:59.300 --> 05:02.460
that's the same thing as
the absolute value of 2a.

05:02.460 --> 05:04.800
So on the left-hand
side right over here,

05:04.800 --> 05:08.930
this is just going to be the
absolute value of 2x minus 10.

05:08.930 --> 05:11.180
And it's going to be less
than on the right-hand side,

05:11.180 --> 05:14.010
you just end up with a 2 delta.

05:14.010 --> 05:16.140
Now, what do we have here
on the left-hand side?

05:16.140 --> 05:19.740
Well, this is f of x as
long as x does not equal 5.

05:19.740 --> 05:21.070
And this is our limit.

05:21.070 --> 05:28.570
So we can rewrite this as f of
x minus L is less than 2 delta.

05:28.570 --> 05:34.210
And this is for x
does not equal 5.

05:34.210 --> 05:37.850
This is f of x, this
literally is our limit.

05:37.850 --> 05:39.540
Now this is interesting.

05:39.540 --> 05:41.690
This statement right
over here is almost

05:41.690 --> 05:43.650
exactly what we want
right over here,

05:43.650 --> 05:46.490
except the right sides
are just different.

05:46.490 --> 05:49.730
In terms of epsilon, this
has it in terms of delta.

05:49.730 --> 05:56.050
So, how can we define delta
so that 2 delta is essentially

05:56.050 --> 05:57.854
going to be epsilon?

05:57.854 --> 05:58.895
Well, this is our chance.

06:03.790 --> 06:07.420
And this is where we're defining
delta as a function of epsilon.

06:07.420 --> 06:10.600
We're going to make 2
delta equal epsilon.

06:10.600 --> 06:12.490
Or if you divide
both sides by 2,

06:12.490 --> 06:16.535
we're going to make delta
equal to epsilon over 2.

06:22.310 --> 06:24.840
Let me switch colors just
to ease the monotony.

06:24.840 --> 06:32.132
If you make delta
equal epsilon over 2,

06:32.132 --> 06:33.590
then this statement
right over here

06:33.590 --> 06:37.530
becomes the absolute
value of f of x minus L

06:37.530 --> 06:40.150
is less than,
instead of 2 delta,

06:40.150 --> 06:42.730
it'll be less than 2
times epsilon over 2.

06:42.730 --> 06:45.360
It's just going to
be less than epsilon.

06:45.360 --> 06:46.800
So this is the key.

06:46.800 --> 06:49.510
If someone gives you any
positive number epsilon

06:49.510 --> 06:53.970
for this function, as long as
you make delta equal epsilon

06:53.970 --> 06:59.060
over 2, then any x
within that range,

06:59.060 --> 07:01.610
that corresponding
f of x is going

07:01.610 --> 07:04.615
to be within epsilon
of our limit.

07:07.845 --> 07:10.220
And remember, it has to be
true for any positive epsilon.

07:10.220 --> 07:12.020
But you could see how
the game could go.

07:12.020 --> 07:15.360
If someone gives
you the epsilon,

07:15.360 --> 07:18.270
let's say they want to be
within 0.5 of our limit.

07:18.270 --> 07:20.770
So our limit is up here,
so our epsilon is 0.5.

07:20.770 --> 07:23.790
So it would literally be the
range I want to be between 10

07:23.790 --> 07:26.160
plus epsilon would be 10.5.

07:26.160 --> 07:29.340
And then 10 minus
epsilon would be 9.5.

07:29.340 --> 07:31.270
Well, we just came
up with a formula.

07:31.270 --> 07:34.170
We just have to make
delta equal to epsilon

07:34.170 --> 07:37.380
over 2, which is equal to 0.25.

07:37.380 --> 07:44.990
So that'll give us a range
between 4.75 and 5.25.

07:44.990 --> 07:51.040
And as long as we pick an
x between 4.75 and 5.25,

07:51.040 --> 07:53.510
but not x equals 5, the
corresponding f of x

07:53.510 --> 07:57.090
will be between 9.5 and 10.5.

07:57.090 --> 07:59.120
And so, you give
me any epsilon, I

07:59.120 --> 08:02.617
can just apply this
formula right over here

08:02.617 --> 08:03.700
to come up with the delta.

08:03.700 --> 08:05.550
This would apply
for any real number.

08:05.550 --> 08:07.350
I mean, especially
any positive number.

08:07.350 --> 08:09.350
For any epsilon
you give me, I just

08:09.350 --> 08:12.730
get a delta defined
this way, and then I

08:12.730 --> 08:16.490
can go through this arc.

08:16.490 --> 08:19.170
If the absolute value of x
minus 5 is less than delta,

08:19.170 --> 08:21.990
if delta is defined
in this way, which

08:21.990 --> 08:23.880
I could define for
any epsilon, then it

08:23.880 --> 08:26.130
will be the case
that f of x will

08:26.130 --> 08:29.730
be within epsilon of our limit.
